    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Deformation bands typically have lower or higher permeability than the surrounding undeformed sandstone. Based on petrographic and SEM observations, we suggest that the hydraulic properties of deformation bands depend on three sets of parameters: (1) sandstone composition, grain size, and sorting; (2) loading conditions; and (3) diagenesis. Examples from a variety of depositional, structural, and burial environments are presented that emphasize the significance of these parameters and the processes of permeability reduction or increase that control the sealing properties of deformation bands. Such processes include mechanical compaction and dilation, grain size reduction, the preferred alignment of elongate grains and clay minerals, the precipitation of cement within and adjacent to deformation bands, and the creation of secondary porosity and permeability by dissolution processes. It is proposed that many of these controlling parameters are predictable for a given depositional, structural, and burial setting.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
